<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walls of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">completed 413 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Theodosian Walls</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> were the fortifications protecting the Byzantine capital, above all the land walls known as the Theodosian Walls, completed in 413 CE. Their triple line of moat, outer wall and inner wall made the city almost impregnable for centuries. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> treat them as central to the empire's survival, until they were breached by Ottoman artillery in 1453.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Walls of Constantinople" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodosian_Walls_of_Constantinople.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, according to tradition, was founded by Greek colonists from Megara led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a> around 658 BCE, and it was enclosed by a small wall of twenty-seven towers. The researcher Raymond Janin thinks the wall known from late sources reflects the city as rebuilt by the Spartan general Pausanias after 479 BCE. Tombstones were used to repair it in 340 BCE against an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>.</p><p>Constantine the Great rebuilt the city from 324 to 336 and inaugurated it on 11 May 330 as New Rome. His wall, a single line reinforced with towers, ran about 2.8 kilometres west of the earlier Severan wall. It was begun in 324 and completed under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> (r. 337-361). By the early 5th century the city had already spread beyond it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Theodosian walls lie about 2 kilometres west of the Constantinian wall and take their name from Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> (r. 402-450). The work was done in two phases, the first under Anthemius, praetorian prefect of the East, and a law in the Codex Theodosianus shows it finished in 413. An inscription found in 1993 records nine years of work, which suggests that building began around 404 or 405.</p><p>Earthquakes on 25 September 437 and 6 November 447 damaged the walls, the second destroying 57 towers. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila the Hun</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> had them restored in 60 days, using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-racing factions as labour. Most scholars date the outer wall and the wide moat to this repair, though the outer wall may have been part of the original plan.</p><p>The Blachernae walls join the Theodosian line to the sea wall at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>. They are 12 to 15 metres high and thicker than the Theodosian walls. The Golden Horn wall ran 5,600 metres, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Propontis</a> wall almost 8,460 metres, with thirteen gates and 188 towers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The garrison was small: the imperial guards and the city watch under the urban prefect. Threats were met by the field armies in the provinces, and in emergencies such as the earthquake of 447 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and hippodrome factions were conscripted. The walls saved the city during sieges by the Avar-Sassanian coalition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, Rus' and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, among others.</p><p>Further defences stood beyond the city, including the 56-kilometre Anastasian Wall, built in the mid-5th century about 65 kilometres to the west and abandoned in the 7th. Before the last Ottoman assault, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> built Rumelihisarı on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> in just over four months in 1452. Restoration began in the 1980s with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> support, but it was criticised, and the World Monuments Fund placed the walls on its 2008 Watch List.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walls of Constantinople” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Theodosian Walls</b>, whose main line was completed in 413 CE under Theodosius II and which were strengthened after an earthquake in 447 with an outer wall and moat, made the city effectively impregnable to any army that lacked both command of the surrounding waters and the means…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Constantinople had shorter frontiers, a richer and more urban tax base in Anatolia, Syria, and Egypt that the invaders of the fifth century could not reach, and <b>land walls</b> that no army breached until 1453.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>A powerful earthquake destroyed large parts of the <b>Walls of Constantinople</b>, including 57 towers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-6">6 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
